%S A400635 13820,110954,427580,1328456,3476110,8294446,18057566,37282532,
%T A400635 72489674,135922868,244169906,426899404,722325334,1196729072,
%U A400635 1931380856,3064584562,4758812456,7286433988,10955649610,16277801502,23811490298,34478030584,49251158414,69732024240,97561060990
%N A400635 Number of semi-meanders with n crossings and winding number n-14, for n >= 14.
%C A400635 A semi-meander is a semi-infinite, self-avoiding, directed curve that crosses a line one or more times.
%C A400635 The winding number of a semi-meander is the hypothetical minimum number of self-crossings that would be created if a ray were to start at the source of the semi-infinite directed curve and extend to infinity without crossing the line.
%C A400635 The maximum winding number of a semi-meander with n crossings is n-2. Thus a(n) counts semi-meanders whose winding number is 12 less than the maximum.
%C A400635 Equivalent to a closed curve (a loop) transversely crossing a ray, considered up to continuous deformation fixing the ray, with a winding number equal to the number of crossings that would be created if the fixed end of the ray were extended to form a line.
%C A400635 Analogized to a river (a semi-meander, meander, or loop) crossing a road (a ray, line, or line segment).
%C A400635 The seventh diagonal of A400429, reading diagonals from the maximal-winding edge.
%H A400635 Hunter Hogan, <a href="/A400635/b400635.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 14..10013</a>
%H A400635 Philippe Di Francesco, Olivier Golinelli and Emmanuel Guitter, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9607039">Meanders: A Direct Enumeration Approach</a>, arXiv:hep-th/9607039, 1996 (preprint).
%H A400635 Philippe Di Francesco, Olivier Golinelli and Emmanuel Guitter, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0550-3213(96)00505-6">Meanders: A Direct Enumeration Approach</a>, Nuclear Physics B 482 (1996), no. 3, 497-535.
%H A400635 Hunter Hogan, <a href="https://github.com/hunterhogan/mapFolding">mapFolding: Create, alter, and optimize algorithms to count meanders, stamp foldings, and map foldings</a>, GitHub repository, 2026.
%H A400635 Joe Sawada and Roy Li, <a href="https://doi.org/10.37236/2404">Stamp Foldings, Semi-Meanders, and Open Meanders: Fast Generation Algorithms</a>, The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, Volume 19 No. 2 (2012), #P43 (16 pages).
%F A400635 a(n) = T(n,floor(n/2)-6), where T is the triangle A400429.
%F A400635 More generally, if D_j is the j-th diagonal of A400429, then 2*j is its least index.
%F A400635 For this sequence, j = 7 and 2*j = 14.
%F A400635 D_j(2*j)   = a(14) = A005315(j)   = A005316(2*j-1).
%F A400635 D_j(2*j+1) = a(15) = A005315(j+1) = A005316(2*j+1) = D_{j+1}(2*j+2) = A400636(16).
%e A400635 Row n=18 of A400429 is 933458,3807200,3476110,1584952,408156,59736,4682,170,2, corresponding to winding numbers 0,2,4,6,8,10,12,14,16. Since n-14=4, a(18)=3476110, which is the seventh-to-last value.
%Y A400635 Seventh diagonal of A400429. Cf. A005315, A005316, A400630, A400631, A400632, A400633, A400634, A400636, A400637, A400638.
%K A400635 nonn,new
